{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Changes on feature/specs-77-79-80-integration",
  "description": "This Changes Tour follows the committed branch from the merge-base of upstream/main to HEAD; uncommitted changes are excluded. It moves from standalone playback through the shared MCP generation boundary and its thin launch adapters.\n\n**Diagram — Reader and generation boundaries**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  User[Reader or author] --> CLI[Standalone CLI]\n  CLI --> HTML[HTML reader]\n  CLI --> TUI[TermDOM reader]\n  Adapter[Copilot adapter] --> CLI\n  Adapter --> Agent[Host agent]\n  Agent --> MCP[Shared MCP server]\n  MCP --> Tours[Tour files and Git snapshots]\n  CLI --> Tours\n```\n\nCritical review found one heuristic Standards risk in terminal layout and one Spec acceptance gap: issue #79's required real Windows speech smoke evidence remains pending. No other review findings were established.\n\nGenerated from the merge-base of `upstream/main` (`29955a004445d3fd2ad5df35dd00f9cc0400ba7d`) to `eff16229ea810df554c72e469d01ca666cbc79ae`.",
  "ref": "eff16229ea810df554c72e469d01ca666cbc79ae",
  "baseRef": "29955a004445d3fd2ad5df35dd00f9cc0400ba7d",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Orienting in the standalone reader",
      "description": "The branch adds an autonomous command-line reader with HTML and TermDOM Playback Surfaces, while preserving the existing Tour files and shared generation engine. The following steps trace how the CLI discovers Tours, resolves their source context, and reaches the generation adapters.",
      "directory": "src/cli"
    },
    {
      "title": "Dispatching reader and MCP commands",
      "description": "The standalone entry recognizes the reserved `mcp` command before reader setup, so MCP startup does not depend on Git-based Tour discovery. Without that token, the CLI selects the existing Tour-reading path and renderer; the dispatch regression coverage is in `src/cli/cli.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/index.ts",
      "pattern": "if \\(process\\.argv\\[2\\] === \"mcp\"\\)",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Opening Tours in standalone HTML",
      "description": "The HTML renderer builds a self-contained playback document that presents the selected step and supports previous/next/finish navigation without requiring VS Code. Its browser-side step state remains in memory; coverage is in `src/cli/html.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/html.ts",
      "pattern": "function show\\(\\)\\{",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Resolving code and persisted diffs",
      "description": "The reader resolves a step’s code context from the workspace or the Tour’s referenced commit, and reads Changes Tour diffs from the persisted base/head snapshots. This keeps the displayed comparison tied to the saved Tour rather than substituting the current working tree; coverage is in `src/cli/stepContext.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/stepContext.ts",
      "pattern": "function resolveDiff\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Navigating the TermDOM reader",
      "description": "The terminal surface renders Tour steps and handles keyboard navigation, scrolling, progress, and completion. The current step is reader state rather than a write to the `.tour` file; coverage is in `src/cli/terminal.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/terminal.ts",
      "pattern": "export async function runTerminalTour\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Failing explicitly on unsupported content",
      "description": "The TermDOM path converts supported descriptions for terminal display and raises a typed rendering error when a reached step contains unsupported content. It does not silently skip that step or switch to another renderer; related coverage is in `src/cli/terminalContent.test.ts` and `src/cli/terminal.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/terminalContent.ts",
      "pattern": "renderTerminalDescription",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Rendering Mermaid for terminals",
      "description": "The terminal reader sends Mermaid source through its optional terminal rendering path instead of assuming the VS Code SVG/PNG surface is available. If rendering is unavailable, the terminal path returns no drawing; tests are in `src/cli/termaid.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/termaid.ts",
      "pattern": "export async function renderMermaidText\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Validating Tour image assets",
      "description": "The MCP boundary constrains local Tour image references to the reserved image area and validates the referenced content before persistence. The shared renderer then makes accepted image data available to playback; validation coverage is in `packages/mcp-server/test/integration/image-validation.test.ts`.",
      "file": "packages/mcp-server/src/image-validation.ts",
      "pattern": "tourImageDirectoryName",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Launching an independent terminal window",
      "description": "The terminal launcher opens a separate OS terminal for the TermDOM reader, leaving it independent of the process that requested playback. When a supported graphical terminal host is unavailable, it reports that condition rather than relying on a multiplexer; coverage includes `src/cli/terminal.test.ts` and `test/standalone-terminal-smoke.py`.",
      "file": "src/cli/terminalLauncher.ts",
      "pattern": "export async function launchTerminalReader\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Grounding Step conversations",
      "description": "Standalone Step conversations keep their exchanges in memory per Tour Step and build question context from the displayed code or paired diff snapshots. The same context boundary serves the reader without changing persisted Tour descriptions; tests are in `src/cli/stepChatSupport.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/stepChatSupport.ts",
      "pattern": "buildStepChatContext\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Preparing descriptions for narration",
      "description": "The narration preparer converts a Step’s Markdown description to spoken text in source order, replacing Mermaid, image, and other code fences with short spoken cues. It does not narrate the code pane or invent text for an empty description; coverage is in `src/cli/narration/prepareText.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/narration/prepareText.ts",
      "pattern": "export function prepareNarrationText\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Controlling narration across navigation",
      "description": "The narration controller owns the enabled state and cancels/replaces speech as the active Step changes. The reader commits narration only for a successfully displayed Step, preventing a delayed callback from speaking stale navigation state; coverage is in `src/cli/narration/NarrationController.test.ts` and `src/cli/narration/readerNarration.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/narration/NarrationController.ts",
      "pattern": "onStepChanged\\(description: string\\): void",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Using local operating-system speech",
      "description": "The optional speech adapter uses the installed macOS `say` command or Windows System.Speech through PowerShell, while keeping synthesis outside TermDOM and the agent. Missing or failed speech is reported without disabling reading; deterministic adapter coverage is in `src/cli/narration/SystemSpeech.test.ts`.",
      "file": "src/cli/narration/SystemSpeech.ts",
      "pattern": "export function createSystemSpeech\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Checking real speech acceptance evidence",
      "description": "The opt-in real-voice smoke test is macOS-specific; the validation ledger records real Windows voice behavior as pending. **Spec:** Issue [maurice30120/codetour#79](https://github.com/maurice30120/codetour/issues/79) requires: “Feasibility and interruption behavior must be smoke-tested on real Windows and macOS terminals before claiming cross-platform support.” The current evidence does not establish that Windows acceptance criterion.",
      "file": "src/cli/narration/saySmoke.test.ts",
      "pattern": "real macOS voice speaks an utterance to completion",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Formatting the terminal status row",
      "description": "The terminal layout helpers fit status text to the available width from either end, so the voice indicator can coexist with navigation and help hints. **Standards:** Risk / code smell (possible Duplicated Code, heuristic): `fit` and `tail` both use `const chars = Array.from(text); if (chars.length <= width) return text;` before applying an ellipsis, although they truncate in opposite directions; this is a possible maintenance risk, not a demonstrated standards violation.",
      "file": "src/cli/terminalLayout.ts",
      "pattern": "function fit\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Sharing the MCP command entry",
      "description": "The MCP package exposes one programmatic command entry for argument parsing and stdio startup, including workspace selection and startup diagnostics. The npm binary and standalone `codetour mcp` command use this shared entry; process-boundary tests are in `packages/mcp-server/test/integration/cli.test.ts`.",
      "file": "packages/mcp-server/src/cli.ts",
      "pattern": "export async function runMcpCli\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Preserving Tour generation and Git comparison",
      "description": "Project and Changes Tour proposals pass through MCP validation and the shared persistence path. For committed Changes Tours, the server verifies the supplied full head SHA, resolves `baseRef`, computes the merge-base, checks anchors against the analyzed commit, and writes the default `.tours/changes.tour` only after validation; these contracts are exercised in `packages/mcp-server/test/integration/changes-tour.test.ts`.",
      "file": "packages/mcp-server/src/server.ts",
      "pattern": "resolveCommit\\(ctx, baseRef\\)",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Selecting neutral or critical review",
      "description": "The Changes Tour input accepts the optional `neutral` or `critical` review mode while retaining the existing tool and `.tour` format. Critical mode is generation guidance only; it is not persisted in the Tour, and the MCP server still validates anchors and writes the proposal; coverage is in `packages/mcp-server/test/integration/critical-review.test.ts`.",
      "file": "packages/mcp-server/src/server.ts",
      "pattern": "reviewMode: z",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Serving the offline review skill pack",
      "description": "The MCP resource assembles the separate CodeTour adaptation, upstream review skill, source revision, attribution, and MIT license for offline clients. The generated module is tested against its source documents in `packages/mcp-server/test/unit/review-skill.test.ts`.",
      "file": "packages/mcp-server/src/review-skill.ts",
      "pattern": "export function criticalReviewSkillPack\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Keeping the Copilot adapter thin",
      "description": "The adapter gathers Read/Generate and Project/Changes choices, reads the critical instructions before forwarding a critical request, and asks the host agent to call the shared MCP tool. It leaves Tour analysis, validation, and persistence in the shared CLI/MCP implementation; the fake-host contract is covered by `test/copilot-adapter.test.mjs`.",
      "file": ".github/extensions/codetour/adapter.mjs",
      "pattern": "session.send\\(\\{prompt, mode: \"immediate\"\\}\\)",
      "display": "diff"
    },
    {
      "title": "Packaging one standalone MCP implementation",
      "description": "The standalone package includes the MCP implementation within the CLI bundle and checks that it is not duplicated as a second server entry. The packager also enforces its measured bundle budget; artifact-level coverage is in `test/standalone-package.test.js`.",
      "file": "scripts/package-standalone.js",
      "pattern": "function assertSingleMcpServer\\(",
      "display": "diff"
    }
  ]
}
